  "result": "Default `[]` is built once, when `def` runs, not per call. Both calls get the same list object, so `add(2)` appends to the list left by `add(1)`.\n\nFix: use `None` as sentinel, make new list per call.\n\n```python\ndef add(x, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(x)\n    return items\n```\n\nNow `add(1)` returns `[1]`, `add(2)` returns `[2]`.\n\nSame bug hits `dict`, `set`, any mutable default. Immutable defaults (`int`, `str`, `tuple`, `None`) are safe.",
